<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Kii Domain</h1><p class="st-meta">1619 CE – 1871 CE · capital Wakayama</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">One of the three Tokugawa branch houses, ruling from Wakayama, which gave Japan two shoguns, Yoshimune and Iemochi.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1619 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> moved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-yorinobu">Tokugawa Yorinobu</a>, the tenth son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a>, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wakayama">Wakayama</a>, giving him Kii province and part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ise-province">Ise</a> with about 555,000 koku. Kii became one of the three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gosanke">Gosanke</a> houses entitled to supply a shogun.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The domain controlled the Kii peninsula, with forests of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumano">Kumano</a> region, mandarin orange orchards, fisheries and the soy sauce makers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuasa-wakayama">Yuasa</a>. When the main line of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shoguns</a> ended in 1716, the Kii lord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-yoshimune">Tokugawa Yoshimune</a> became the eighth shogun and launched the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoho-reforms">Kyoho reforms</a>. In 1858 another Kii lord, Yoshitomi, became Shogun <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-iemochi">Tokugawa Iemochi</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Kii submitted to the new government in 1868 and carried out military reforms. After abolition in 1871 it became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wakayama-prefecture">Wakayama Prefecture</a>; the diplomat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mutsu-munemitsu">Mutsu Munemitsu</a> came from Kii.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kii-domain.jpg" alt="Kii Domain" width="320" height="249"><figcaption>Image: Barfodt · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Tokugawa_Lords_of_Wakayama_in_Kii_Province_(A).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1619 CE – 1871 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Wakayama</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Tokugawa Yorinobu, Tokugawa Yoshimune, Tokugawa Iemochi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism, Shinto</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Japanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Wakayama Domain, Kishu Domain</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1745&amp;focus=kii-domain" aria-label="Kii Domain on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8120 937 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kii Domain lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8440 1193L8530 1145M8440 1193L8529 1145"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8530 1145h0M8529 1145h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8440 1193h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8440 1193h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Wakayama. <a href="/atlas?year=1745&amp;focus=kii-domain">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
